Each log line on its way out at once. Mercury 1.9.21 writes its log lines to stderr with fprintf (common/hermes_log.c, print_entry) and never flushes stderr. The C library of Linux and macOS
leaves stderr unbuffered, so the daemon reads each line as it is written; the C runtime of Windows buffers stderr when it is a pipe, as it is under NeoAPRS, so there the lines came only when the
buffer filled or the program ended. The line that says the sound card could not be opened (neoaprs/mercury.py: audio_open_error), which Mercury writes and then runs on without the card, reached the
daemon 20 minutes late on a Windows test station (3 November 2026), when Mercury ended on the next frame; the HF radio showed connected all that time. This adds one fflush(stderr) after each line.
Mercury 1.9.22 writes the same way (checked 4 November 2026).

scripts/build-mercury applies this file after dsound.patch (`patch -p1`) on every system and checks that the source holds the flush; made from the source of the pinned commit (neoaprs/mercury.py:
SOURCE_COMMIT), so a new pin that moves these lines fails the build loudly, which is right.

--- a/common/hermes_log.c
+++ b/common/hermes_log.c
@@ -217,6 +217,7 @@
         if (dropped > 0)
             fprintf(stderr, "%s %s [WRN] [log] dropped %lu messages\n",
                     tsbuf, upbuf, dropped);
+        fflush(stderr);
     }
 
     if (entry->to_file)
